{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}
{% block body_class %}user-guide{% endblock %}
{% block title %}User Guide – Settings & Administration{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
{% set section_title = 'Settings & Administration' %}
{{ include('user_guide/_breadcrumb.html.twig') }}

<div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center mb-4">
    <h1 class="h3 mb-0"><i class="bi bi-gear me-2"></i>Settings &amp; Administration</h1>
</div>

<div class="row g-4">
<div class="col-lg-9">

<div class="card mb-4" id="buildings">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-building me-1"></i>Buildings</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Buildings are physical location labels you can assign to hosts. Manage them at <strong>Other Settings → Buildings</strong>.</p>
        <p>Each building has a name (e.g. <code>Science Hall</code>). When adding a host, you can select a building and enter a room number. This location data is searchable from the host list — useful for quickly finding all devices in a specific building or room.</p>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">Buildings are optional. If your organization doesn't track physical locations, you can ignore them entirely.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="tags">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-tags me-1"></i>Tags</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Tags are free-form labels you can apply to hosts and subnets for grouping and filtering. Manage them at <strong>Other Settings → Tags</strong>.</p>
        <p>Examples of useful tags: <code>lab</code>, <code>production</code>, <code>iot</code>, <code>wifi</code>, <code>managed</code>, <code>unmanaged</code>.</p>
        <p>Tags can be applied to hosts individually (via the host edit form or the bulk-tag operation on the host list) and to subnets (via the subnet edit form). The host advanced search filter lets you show only hosts with a specific tag.</p>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">Tags are shared across the whole application — the same tag can be applied to both hosts and subnets.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="app-settings">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-sliders me-1"></i>Application Settings</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Configure global application behavior at <strong>Other Settings → Application Settings</strong>.</p>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><th colspan="2" class="table-light small text-muted">General</th></tr>
                    <tr><td>Display Timezone</td><td>Timezone used for scheduled task evaluation and timestamps throughout the UI</td></tr>
                    <tr><th colspan="2" class="table-light small text-muted">Retention</th></tr>
                    <tr><td>Default DHCP Lease Retention (days)</td><td>How many days DHCP lease records are kept. Older records are purged by the scheduled task.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Push Log Retention (days)</td><td>How many days push log entries are kept before being purged</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Deleted Host/Interface Retention (days)</td><td>How many days soft-deleted hosts and interfaces are kept before being permanently purged. Individual subnets can override this.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Default Retention for New Subnets (days)</td><td>Default lease retention applied when a new subnet is created (can be changed per-subnet)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>ClearPass Auth Log Retention (days)</td><td>How many days ClearPass auth log entries are kept before being purged</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Switch Info Max Age (days)</td><td>How long cached switch port info is considered fresh before being re-fetched from the switch</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Activity Log Retention (days)</td><td>How many days activity log entries are kept. Leave blank to keep entries indefinitely.</td></tr>
                    <tr><th colspan="2" class="table-light small text-muted">SMTP</th></tr>
                    <tr><td>SMTP Host / Port</td><td>Outbound mail server hostname and port for sending notification emails</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Encryption</td><td>TLS mode: None, STARTTLS, or TLS/SSL</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>SMTP Username / Password</td><td>Credentials for authenticated SMTP (leave blank for unauthenticated relay)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>From Address / From Name</td><td>Sender address and display name for outbound emails. Use <strong>Send Test Email</strong> to verify configuration.</td></tr>
                    <tr><th colspan="2" class="table-light small text-muted">Remote Syslog</th></tr>
                    <tr><td>Enable remote syslog forwarding</td><td>When enabled, every activity log entry is forwarded asynchronously to a syslog server in RFC 5424 format, including field-level change details</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Syslog Server Host</td><td>Hostname or IP of the syslog server to receive forwarded log entries</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Syslog Port</td><td>UDP or TCP port the syslog server listens on (default: 514)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Syslog Protocol</td><td>UDP or TCP. TCP connections are kept alive across messages by the background worker.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="backup">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-database-down me-1"></i>Backup &amp; Restore</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI creates encrypted database backups that you can restore if needed. Manage backups at <strong>Other Settings → Backup &amp; Restore</strong>.</p>
        <h6>Creating backups</h6>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Manual</strong> — click <strong>Backup Now</strong> on the Backup page</li>
            <li><strong>Scheduled</strong> — configure a cron schedule in <strong>Other Settings → Scheduler</strong> for the Database Backup task</li>
        </ul>
        <h6>What's included</h6>
        <p>Each backup is a MySQL dump of the entire application database. Options in Backup Settings control how many backups to keep (<strong>Backups to Keep</strong> — older files are deleted automatically), whether to encrypt the file (AES-256-GCM + PBKDF2), and whether to embed the <code>APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code> in the backup so encrypted fields (SSH keys, passwords) remain readable after a restore to a different server.</p>
        <p>Three high-volume tables can be excluded to keep backup files smaller: <strong>DHCP lease log</strong>, <strong>Activity log</strong>, and <strong>ClearPass auth logs</strong>. Enable the corresponding checkboxes in Backup Settings if this data is not needed for a restore.</p>
        <h6>Restoring a backup</h6>
        <ol class="mb-0">
            <li>On the Backup page, click <strong>Restore</strong> next to the backup you want, or upload a backup file from another server</li>
            <li>If the backup includes an <code>APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code>, DashDDI will show it in a banner — update your server's <code>.env.local</code> file with the key before using the app</li>
            <li>Confirm the restore — all current data will be replaced by the backup</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="api-tokens">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-key me-1"></i>API Tokens</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>API tokens allow external scripts and tools to authenticate to DashDDI's REST API. There are two kinds: <strong>general tokens</strong> (managed under <strong>Other Settings → API Tokens</strong>) and <strong>host-scoped tokens</strong> (generated from the Host detail page).</p>

        <h6>General tokens</h6>
        <ol>
            <li>Go to <strong>Other Settings → API Tokens</strong> and click <strong>New Token</strong></li>
            <li>Enter a name that identifies what this token is used for (e.g. <code>monitoring-tool</code>)</li>
            <li>Save — the token value is shown <strong>once only</strong>. Copy it immediately.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Each general token has granular per-route permissions. Click <strong>Edit Permissions</strong> to toggle access to individual API endpoints. By default, new tokens have no access — you must explicitly grant the routes they need. You can also restrict a general token to a list of allowed source IP addresses or CIDR ranges.</p>

        <h6 id="host-scoped-tokens">Host-scoped tokens</h6>
        <p>A host-scoped token is a special token tied to a single host. It can <strong>only</strong> access the <code>/api/self/*</code> endpoints, and only from the IP addresses on that host's own network interfaces — no manual IP configuration needed.</p>
        <p>Use these when a host needs to manage its own DNS records for ACME (DNS-01) certificate validation. Each host may have at most one token at a time.</p>
        <h6 class="mt-3">Managing host-scoped tokens</h6>
        <ul>
            <li>Open a Host's detail page and scroll to the <strong>Host API Token</strong> card</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Generate Token</strong> — the raw token value is shown once and then cleared. Copy it immediately.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Regenerate Token</strong> at any time to invalidate the current token and issue a new one</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Revoke Token</strong> to permanently delete the token</li>
        </ul>
        <p>You can also generate and revoke host tokens via the API (<code>POST /api/hosts/{id}/token</code> and <code>DELETE /api/hosts/{id}/token</code>) — see the <a href="{{ path('user_guide_servers') }}#api">Servers &amp; Integrations guide → REST API</a> for details.</p>

        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">All tokens are used in the <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> HTTP header. General tokens can be regenerated at any time by editing them from the API Tokens page. The API Tokens list shows general tokens first, followed by all host-scoped tokens grouped by host.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="saml">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-shield-lock me-1"></i>SAML Authentication</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI authenticates users via SAML 2.0. Manage identity provider (IdP) configurations at <strong>Other Settings → SAML Providers</strong>.</p>
        <h6>Adding a SAML provider</h6>
        <ol>
            <li>In your IdP (e.g. Okta, OneLogin, Azure AD, Shibboleth), create a new SAML application. The <strong>ACS (Assertion Consumer Service) URL</strong> and <strong>Entity ID</strong> are available from the DashDDI SAML provider page.</li>
            <li>In DashDDI, add a SAML provider and paste the IdP metadata XML (or provide the metadata URL)</li>
            <li>Save — users can now log in via SAML at the DashDDI login page</li>
        </ol>
        <h6>Attribute mapping</h6>
        <p class="mb-0">DashDDI reads the SAML <code>NameID</code> as the user identifier and expects <code>email</code> and <code>displayName</code> (or <code>cn</code>) attributes from the IdP. Configure your IdP application to send these attributes in the SAML assertion.</p>
        <div class="alert alert-info mt-3 mb-0 py-2 small">
            <i class="bi bi-lightbulb me-1"></i>The SAML metadata import console command (<code>app:saml:import-idp-metadata</code>) can import IdP metadata directly from a URL — useful during initial setup before a SAML provider is configured in the UI.
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="themes">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-palette2 me-1"></i>Themes</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI supports multiple color themes. Click the palette icon (<i class="bi bi-palette2"></i>) in the top-right navbar to switch between:</p>
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li><strong>Light</strong> — standard Bootstrap light theme</li>
            <li><strong>Dark</strong> — dark background for reduced eye strain</li>
            <li><strong>Purple</strong> — dark theme with purple accents</li>
            <li><strong>Green</strong> — dark theme with green accents</li>
            <li><strong>Rainbow</strong> — animated multi-color theme</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-2 mb-0 text-muted small">Your theme preference is saved per-user automatically.</p>
    </div>
</div>

</div>{# /col-lg-9 #}

